{"id": "d773e14a-89eb-493c-99e0-0f12bddf34a5", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T20:11:54.408335", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "990a1005-7022-4ee3-87de-17540d3c6385", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-90e1552d685f3511", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"python3 -c \\\"import sys; print(sys.version)\\\"; echo ---; python3 -m pip list 2>/dev/null | head -30\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.366, \"output\": \"3.14.4 (main, Jun 18 2026, 14:25:02) [GCC 15.2.0]\\n---\\nPackage                   Version\\n------------------------- -------------\\nansible                   13.1.0\\nansible-core              2.20.1\\napache-libcloud           3.8.0\\naptdaemon                 2.0.2\\nargcomplete               3.6.3\\nattrs                     25.4.0\\nautocommand               2.2.2\\nbabel                     2.17.0\\nbcc                       0.35.0\\nbcrypt                    5.0.0\\nblinker                   1.9.0\\nBrlapi                    0.8.6\\ncertifi                   2026.1.4\\nchardet                   5.2.0\\nclick                     8.1.8\\ncommand-not-found         0.3\\nconfigobj                 5.0.9\\ncryptography              46.0.5\\ncupshelpers               1.0\\ndasbus                    1.7\\ndbus-python               1.4.0\\ndecorator                 5.2.1\\ndefer                     1.0.6\\ndistro                    1.9.0\\ndistro-info               1.15\\ndnspython                 2.8.0\\ngssapi                    1.10.1\\ngyp-next                  0.16.2\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "0d5822b0-4942-4dca-b78a-65454aa888ae", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "21a98df9-75f4-4e29-b48b-f3749c2e36eb", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T20:12:33.264591", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "d773e14a-89eb-493c-99e0-0f12bddf34a5", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Confirmed: the repository contains only task.json. There are no InfLLM source materials, no paper, no datasets, no torch/transformers. Therefore, for Part 1 of the researcher's request, I have to report \"does not exist\"—since there is no InfLLM code in the workspace, I cannot identify actual code modules (context memory writing, retrieval, unit representation, config items) from the source. I must not fabricate a file structure.\n\nWhat I can offer:\n1. Honest inspection results: the repository contains only task.json. Nothing else. No InfLLM code, no datasets, GPU is disabled, no project dependencies (torch/transformers not installed). Network is disabled → cannot fetch the upstream repo.\n2. A proposed approach at the bullet-point rationale level. Based on the publicly known InfLLM design (arXiv 2402.04617, InfLLM: Learning to Retrieve Long-range Context from Cross-layer Memory by Salience)—but I must make explicit that these are assumptions from prior knowledge, not facts verified in the source. Key points of InfLLM: context memory is stored in units of 128-token blocks. Each block is represented by the key-value pairs of its first (4) tokens (a \"few tokens\" representation), with saliency derived from attention scores. Per layer-group, top-k block retrieval (k=20 in the paper). In the original InfLLM, memory is read-only (no update mechanism. The follow-up InfLLM-V2 adds memory update/refinement). The baseline in the paper is a comparison against full attention, sliding window, retrieval-augmentation using an external retriever, and a \"random block\" ablation. Also, the paper itself has ablations on \"number of representation tokens\" (1, 2, 4, 8 tokens), block size, and k.\n\nSince the source is not present, the plan should be as follows: (a) state that inspection found no code. (b) propose a design grounded in the publicly documented InfLLM v1 mechanism, all flagged as assumptions. (c) enumerate what needs to be confirmed by the researcher, or requires obtaining source access.\n\nProposed factorial design:\n- Factors:\n  F1: Dynamism of retrieval — dynamic (per-layer group, per-query top-k via saliency), static/fixed (random or fixed blocks, oracle blocks, single-block retrieval across the whole input), no retrieval (local window only).\n  F2: Unit representation — raw first-N-token KV (InfLLM default N=4), N ∈ {1,2,4,8,16}, mean-pooled KV, cluster/learned summaries, full-block KV (all-128-token representation), and as an upper bound, an oracle \"ground-truth relevant-token\" representation.\n- Anchors/controls: full attention upper bound (e.g., Llama-2-7B-32K / LongChat 32K or YaRN), lower bound = local window only. An external retriever baseline (BM25/E5 chunk retrieval RAG). Random block retrieval. Oracle retrieval (upper bound on retrieval quality). This lets us decompose performance into lookup quality vs. representation loss.\n- Task families: retrieval-limited (multi-key NIAH / long-range QA that requires pinpoint retrieval: InfiniteBench EN.MC/QA, HotpotQA/2WikiMQA at 32k, FLenQA), aggregation (count, word-problem sort/LOFT, novel-order QA requiring many-to-many memory from NoCha/NovelQA), reasoning that needs updates? InfLLM v1 has no updates. Skip. Also a needle-with-question (NWQ) distractor set for lookup specificity.\n- Metrics: exact match / F1 / accuracy per family. Retrieval-level diagnostics: top-k block recall against annotated evidence blocks (requires evidence-span annotation, or NIAH with known needle positions). Sensitivity curves: accuracy vs. k, accuracy vs. N representation tokens, accuracy vs. block size. Attention-mass / saliency correlation between the gold-evidence block and the retrieved blocks. Degradation relative to full attention (normalized \"efficiency gap\").\n- Factorial: 2 factors × 