s plan: per-factor deltas (Δ_lookup, Δ_repr), 3×4 interaction grid, top-k block recall और saliency-rank diagnostics, accuracy-vs-k और accuracy-vs-N curves, two-way ANOVA/mixed-effects + bootstrap CIs, task-family × factor interaction की व्याख्या का decision rule (कौन-सा pattern देखकर हम कहेंगे कि lookup critical है बनाम representation critical है), (d) feasibility: GPU/weights/network की अनुपलब्धता को स्पष्ट execution-prerequisite के रूप में लिखें, इस task में execution required नहीं है — कोई run-claims नहीं। साथ एक explicit limitations section: paper-derived defaults unverified, effect sizes pilot-पूर्व unknown, aggregation-task representation ceiling unquantified।\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "af137918-eb90-4795-99c9-c67f3c9f6f5e", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T20:14:53.046562", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "204efb60-5963-491a-aa03-49deab388af8", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "The researcher has been approved, and is now requesting a complete stage = results design. All details should be woven into the answer of end_researcher_turn with stage=result. No execution needed. Structure:\n\n(a) Rationale and v1 premise framing\n(b) Factors and levels — complete design: Factor A (exploration dynamism), Factor B (unit representation), interaction 3×4 grid, anchors\n(c) Complete control set\n(d) Task family and dataset\n(e) Metrics and diagnostics\n(f) Analysis plan: Δ_lookup, Δ_repr, ANOVA/mixed effects, bootstrap CI, decision rules for interpretation\n(g) Optional third factor: V2-style memory update (with label)\n(h) Feasibility / prerequisites for execution\n(i) Limitations\n(j) Rebinding memo for when the actual code becomes available\n\nEverything must be clearly stated: mechanism values are flagged as \"reported in the paper, unverified\"; base checkpoint is a parametric assumption; 64k is optionally labeled as extrapolation; no claims about execution.\n\nIt should be complete yet concise. Write the answer in a structured format. Language: the researcher uses a mix of Hindi/English; write the design in a mix of English and Hindi — better to maintain technical clarity; write in English interspersed with light Hinglish to match the previous turn, or is full English with Hinglish headings fine? The researcher has no problem writing bilingually; I'll write in bilingual-style English for precision. For the key content, English is better for precision; maintain some Hinglish for consistency. Actually, since precision is needed, keep the headings and explanations in English — the researcher reads English without any problem. I'll take a light bilingual approach.\n\nDecision rules for interpretation:\n- When exploration is critical: dynamic >> random at matched budget, and dynamic ≪ oracle; large Δ_lookup; in that case, the exploration gap dominates the gap to full attention.\n- When representation is critical: accuracy-vs-N curve rises sharply toward full-block, Δ_repr is large; oracle retrieval at N=4 still falls far short of full attention.\n- Coupling: significant interaction — e.g., dynamic's gain over random exists only at N≥8, etc.\n- Pattern by task family: exploration-driven on retrieval-type tasks (Δ_lookup ≫ Δ_repr), representation-driven on aggregation-type tasks (Δ_repr dominates; oracle at small N cannot recover), specificity: distractor tasks show large dynamic-vs-external-retriever differences.\n\nMemory computation and budget: keep reasonable estimates: about 30 runs for 7B model on 32k contexts, per dataset... provide approximate GPU-hour estimates flagged as rough.\n\nAlso configuration table: Factor A levels (6), Factor B levels (7), grid 3×4 = 12 cells; total conditions ≈ A×B full factorial is subsetted: default N under A levels (6), default A under B levels (7) → single-factor sweeps + 3×4 grid = about 12 cells for about 17 unique conditions + 2 anchors. Shared memory bank: Factor B requires recomputation of the memory representation; Factor A reuses the same saliency scores (except oracle/external). Cost note: memory precomputation is one forward pass over the long context per item × N levels; can be computed in a single forward pass and pooled.\n\nMetrics: EM/F1 for QA, accuracy for NIAH/count; per-item binary correctness for ANOVA; diagnostics: top-k block recall (evidence spans → block mapping, hit if the gold block is in the top-k), saliency rank of the gold block (MRR / median rank), attention-mass coverage ratio.\n\nRandom seed: the model is deterministic, decoding is greedy; bootstrap over items; 2 seeds for random-block conditions.\n\nFeasibility: environment without GPU, no torch/transformers, no network, no weights → execution is impossible here; required prerequisites: ≥1×80GB GPU (7B fp16 + 32k KV is heavy; batch 1), HF weights, dataset downloads. This is a design-only task; do not claim execution.\n\nLimitations: defaults unverified, effect sizes unknown, aggregation ceiling unquantified, NIAH may overestimate the importance of exploration, saliency in a single forward pass of the base model without fine-tuning (paper reported with additional pretraining? InfLLM v1 did further pretraining on LongAlign... actually, the paper reported that they pretrain on LongAlign-pretrain 20B tokens under memory; flag this: \"reported in the paper, unverified\" — whether training is needed for memory usage affects the design; if weights are unavailable, use as-is in a training-free mode with the caveat that absolute numbers drop but relative factor contrasts remain the primary readout — state as an assumption). This is a good caveat: since it is checkpoint-agnostic, the contrasts are within-condition differences, which are more robust.\n\nRebinding memo: on code release: block size, number of tokens per unit, k, layer-group interval, saliency aggregation, memory read/write API; verify whether the representation is first-N KV or pooling, and whether top-k is global or per layer-group.\n\nKeep the answer at an appropriate length. In the evidence field, carry over prior inspection + design rationale. Assumptions are separated from the fixed requirements. Fixed requirements from the researcher: v1 as primary, V2 as optional extension; base is a parametric assumption, primary 32K, 64k optional; design only, flag as unverified, rebinding memo.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design — \"How critical are dynamic context memory lookup and unit representation choice to InfLLM's performance across long-context tasks?\"\n\n## 0. Framing and fixed requirements (researcher-resolved)\n- **Primary target: InfLLM v1 (arXiv 2402.04617) documented mechanism.** Every mechanism-level constant below is **[paper-reported, unverified]** — no InfLLM source exists in the repository (workspace contains only `task.json`; filesystem-wide search returned nothing). Design is paper-description-based, design-only; **no experiment is claimed as run**.\n- **Base checkpoint: parametric assumption** — \"frozen 7B-class long-context checkpoint (e.g., LongChat-7B-32K or YaRN-extended Llama-2)\", no fine-tuning beyond what the checkpoint already has. Primary regime = **32K context**; 64K runs are **optional, explicitly labeled extrapolation**.\n- **V2-style memory update/refinement is NOT in the main factorial** — it appears only as a clearly-labeled optional extension (Section 8), with the note that v1 has no refinement.\n\n**Central question operationalized:** decompose InfLLM's efficiency gap to full attention into (A) a **lookup** component (are the right memory units retrieved?) and (B) a **representation** component (are retrieved units faithfully encoded?), plus their **interaction**, across mechanistically distinct task families.\n\n## 1. Factor definitions\n\n### Factor A — Lookup dynamism (memory representation held at default)\n| Level | Description | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| A1 **dynamic-saliency top-k** (default) | retrieve k≈20 blocks per query per layer-group by saliency-attention scores [paper-reported, unverified] | InfLLM as documented |\n| A2 **random blocks, matched budget** | same number of blocks, uniformly random; 2 seeds | dynamism control at equal budget |\n| A3 **fixed-position blocks, matched budget** | first k + last k blocks (recency/primacy static) | static-retrieval control |\n| A4 **oracle retrieval** | feed only the annotated evidence blocks (grounded at gold positions), same budget | lookup-quality ceiling |\n| A5 **external-retriever (chunk-RAG)** | BM25 + E5 dense retriever over 128-token chunks, matched token budget | non-parametric lookup baseline |\n| A6 **no retrieval** | local window only (sliding window of same token budget as A1) | lower bound of the memory system |\n\n### Factor B — Unit representation (lookup held at A1)\n| Level | Description |\n|---|---|\n| B1 N=1 first-token KV | extreme lossy |\n| B2 **N=4 first-token KV (default)** [paper-reported, unverified] | InfLLM default |\n| B3 N=8 first-token KV | mild fidelity increase |\n| B4 N=16 first-token KV | |\n| B5 N=32 first-token KV | half-block |\n| B6 **mean-pooled block KV** | length-matched but smoothed representation |\n| B7 **full-block KV (N=128)** | representation upper bound at block size 128 [paper-reported, unverified] |\n*(optional B8: k-means centroid KV or learned summary tokens — run only if compute permits; exploratory.)*\n\n### Interaction grid (3×4, the key deliverable)\nLookup {A1 dynamic, A2 random, A4 oracle} × Representation {B1 N=1, B2 N=4, B5 N=32, B7 full-block}.\nRationale: the **oracle×N curve** measures pure representation capacity independent of lookup; the **dynamic−random gap as a function of N** tests whether a lossy unit representation destroys the value of dynamic lookup (coupling) or not (independence).\n\n## 2. Anchors and full control set\n- **Upper anchor — full attention:** the same checkpoint family's full-context mode at 32K (e.g., LongChat-7B-32K native attention). All factor effects reported both raw and as **normalized efficiency gap**: `gap = (acc_full − acc_cond)/(acc_full − acc_local)`.\n- **Lower anchor — local window only** (= A6), same token budget.\n- **Budget-matched random/fixed retrieval** (A2/A3) — isolates dynamism from mere extra-token access.\n- **Oracle retrieval** (A4) — separates \"lookup misses\" from \"representation loss\" as causes of any residual gap.\n- **External retriever** (A5) — tests whether InfLLM's learned-saliency lookup beats off-the-shelf retrieval at equal budget.\n- **Matched-memory-token accounting:** every condition logged with exact attended-token count (k×N×layers-groups) so all \"matched budget\" claims are auditable. Note: B-levels change memory tokens read even at fixed k — so B comparisons are *fidelity-vs-cost tradeoffs*, plotted as accuracy-vs-memory-tokens, not budget-matched contrasts.\n\n## 3. Task families and data (3 mechanistically distinct families)\n1. **Pinpoint retrieval:** multi-key NIAH (1–8 needles, questions about needle value) + HotpotQA/2WikiMQA-32K (evidence spans annotated). Lookup-diagnostics native here (needle/evidence position known).\n2. **Aggregation:** count-type (word/phrase counting), NoCha-style sentence-presence, NovelQA-style event aggregation requiring many low-salience units. Representation-budget-sensitive.\n3. **Distractor-heavy associative QA:** FLenQA/NWQ-style (needle-with-question, semantically similar distractors). Tests lookup *specificity*, where dynamic-vs-external contrast is sharpest.\nSizes: ~200 items/family (NIAH: 100 per key-count), each at 16K and 32K contexts; 64K only as a 60-item extrapolation probe on family 1. Evidence→block mapping derived from annotations.\n**Caveat (v1):** InfLLM-paper training recipe (LongAlign-pretrain continuation) is **[paper-reported, unverified]** and weights availability is unknown; primary design is **training-free use of the checkpoint with memory read**. Absolute accuracies may then fall below paper numbers, but all primary readouts are *within-regime contrasts*, robust to the absolute level. If no 32K checkpoint is usable training-free, a documented fallback is a small shared fine-tune applied identically to all conditions (single extra variable, applied to every arm including anchors).\n\n## 4. Protocol summary (fixed per-condition)\nSame frozen checkpoint, same prompt template, greedy decoding, memory computed once per item and reused across lookup arms; representation arms B1–B7 reuse one forward pass with per-N pooling computed offline. Order counterbalancing irrelevant (deterministic). Seeds: {s1, s2} for A2 only; everything else deterministic.\n\n## 5. Metrics and diagnostics\n- **Task:** EM/F1 (QA families), accuracy (NIAH/count/NoCha). Per-item binary correctness for statistical modeling.\n- **Lookup diagnostics:** top-k **block recall** (gold evidence block ∈ retrieved set), gold-block **saliency-rank MRR/median**, retrieved **attention-mass coverage**.\n- **Curves:** accuracy-vs-k (k ∈ {5,10,20,40}, default k≈20 flagged [paper-reported, unverified]); accuracy-vs-N (B1–B7); accuracy-vs-context-length (8K/16K/32K, 64K labeled extrapolation).\n\n## 6. Analysis plan\n- **Per-factor deltas at default:** `Δ_lookup = acc(A1) − acc(A2)` (dynamism value at matched budget) and `Δ_repr = acc(B7) − acc(B2)` (fidelity value under fixed dynamic lookup), per family, with bootstrap 95% CIs (10k item-resamples).\n- **Two-way model:** mixed-effects logistic regression per family: correctness ~ lookup × representation + (1|item) + context-length as covariate; report main effects + interaction with CIs. Follow-up: trend test on accuracy-vs-N, Dunnett-style comparisons to default condition.\n- **Efficiency-gap decomposition:** fraction of full-attention gap explained by lookup failures (oracle−dynamic) vs representation (full-block−default).\n\n### Decision rule (what pattern ⇒ what conclusion)\n1. **Lookup critical:** Δ_lookup large and CIs exclude small effects, dynamic ≪ oracle, and the oracle−dynamic term dominates the residual gap — especially if oracle@N=4 ≈ full attention on family 1. ⇒ Improvements should target retrieval/scoring, not encoding.\n2. **Representation critical:** accuracy-vs-N curve rises steeply to N≥32, Δ_repr dominant; oracle retrieval at N=4 still far below full attention — especially if family 2 shows a hard representation ceiling. ⇒ Improvements should target richer memory units.\n3. **Coupled (both critical):** significant interaction — e.g., dynamic-over-random gain exists only at N≥16 because precise lookup is wasted on lossy units (or vice versa: N only helps once lookup is good). Report interaction magnitude per family as the headline answer to \"how critical.\"\n4. **Task-family pattern:** family 1 dominated by Δ_lookup, family 2 by Δ_repr, family 3 by large A1-vs-A5 gap ⇒ conclusion is *family-conditional*, not global — this itself is a primary deliverable. If instead both deltas are uniformly small relative to anchors (conditions ≈ full attention), conclusion: neither factor is bottleneck in this regime.\n\n## 7. Feasibility / execution prerequisites (explicit; NOT satisfied in this environment)\n- This environment: **no GPU, no torch/transformers, no network, no model weights, no datasets** (verified during inspection) ⇒ execution here is impossible and none is claimed.\n- Real run needs: HF weights (7B long-context ckpt), dataset downloads, ≥1×80GB GPU for 32K single-stream inference + memory precompute. Rough estimate (flagged rough, pilot-unverified): ~1,500 items × ~20 unique conditions × ~1 forward precompute/representation-family + decode ⇒ on the order of **300–600 GPU-hours** for the main factorial; interaction grid reuses caches; extension adds ~30%.\n\n## 8. Optional labeled extension — third factor C (InfLLM-V2-style)\nMemory update/refinement (multi-scan memory consolidation) as factor C ∈ {off (v1 default — refinement absent in v1), on (V2-style)} on a small subset (families 1–3, 64K probe only). Clearly marked **out-of-primary, v2-mechanism, source-unverified**, run only after primary factorial.\n\n## 9. Limitations\n- All mechanism values (block size 128, first-few-token KV, per-layer-group saliency top-k, k≈20, layer-group stride) are **paper-derived and unverified** — no code exists in the workspace to confirm them.\n- **Effect sizes are unknown pre-pilot**; power analysis for ANOVA/mixed model must follow a pilot (~50 items/family); item counts here are provisional.\n- **Aggregation-task representation ceiling is unquantified** — B7 (full-block) may itself be insufficient for family 2, which would floor the representation curve and bound Δ_repr's interpretability.\n- NIAH may **overestimate lookup criticality** (needle ≈ maximal saliency outlier); mitigated by families 2–3 but not eliminated.\n- Training-free checkpoint use may shift absolute numbers (Section 3 caveat); the fallback fine-tune adds one shared variable.\n- Re-bind note: **if actual code becomes available**, re-derive design knobs directly from it — block size, tokens-per-unit N, k and per-layer-group vs global top-k, saliency aggregation formula, layer-group stride, and the memory read/write API — and replace every [paper-reported, unverified] flag with source-verified values before execution.", "evidence": "Repository inspection (this session): /workspace contains only task.json; filesystem-wide find for InfLLM/memory/retrieval code and datasets → empty; pip list → no torch/transformers; environment metadata → network disabled, gpu_enabled=false. Hence mechanism values are cited as [paper-reported, unverified] per researcher's fixed resolution #3. Design structure per researcher approval and three resolutions: (1) v1-primary with V2 as labeled Section-8 extension; (2) checkpoint-declared parametric, 32K primary / 64K extrapolation-labeled; (3) design-only, paper-based, with explicit re-bind note. Analysis components (Δ_lookup/Δ_repr, 3×4 grid, block recall + saliency-rank, accuracy-vs-k/N curves, two-way mixed-effects + bootstrap CIs, decision rules) correspond one-to-one to the approved factorial plan; controls (full attention, local window, random/fixed matched-budget, oracle, external retriever) correspond to approved control set. No command output is presented as experimental evidence; feasibility constraints cite the verified environment limits (no GPU/network/weights).", "assumptions": ["FIXED (researcher-resolved): primary target = InfLLM v1 documented mechanism; V2 refinement only as labeled extension", "FIXED: base checkpoint parametric ('frozen 7B-class long-context checkpoint, e.g. LongChat-7B-32K/YaRN-Llama-2'), 32K primary regime, 64K extrapolation-labeled", "FIXED: design-only, no co